I went for an explicit allowlist of input-types for using this fast path, rather than allowing everything and excluding some, which would look sth like so: 
```python
def _can_skip_expr_engine(
      value: IntoExpr | None,
      n: int,
      dtype: PolarsDataType | None = None,
  ) -> bool:
      if isinstance(n, bool) or n < 0:
          return False

      if isinstance(value, (pl.Expr, pl.Series)):
          return False
      if _check_for_numpy(value) and isinstance(value, (np.generic, np.ndarray)):
          return False

      return not (
          dtype is not None
          and (
              isinstance(dtype, (BaseExtension, DataTypeExpr))
              or (isinstance(dtype, type) and issubclass(dtype, BaseExtension))
              or dtype == Object
          )
      )
```
The reason is that the `pl.lit()` constructor does a ton of instance checks, but at the same time does not always return simple `LiteralExpr`s (e.g. attaches a cast or returns a `Series`). The allowlist forces new additions to be explicitly checked and has easier-to-maintain behaviour in case this constructor changes.

Happy to change to whatever seems better though

